;;; metric-outer-measure.scm -- METRIC OUTER MEASURES: separated sets, the layers of a
;;; closed set, Borel sets are measurable; Lebesgue outer measure on the line is metric.
;;; Agent MEASURE-3-A, 2026-10-09 (scratchpad/triage/MEASURE-3-A.md).  Library: measure.
;;; Helper prefix mom-.  58 theorems, every one `modulo 0' (oracles ineq, arith, crs).
;;; Vocabulary: structure-library/metric-outer-measure.scm (PT-SET-DIST, SET-DIST,
;;; IS-METRIC-OUTER-MEASURE, BOREL-SETS).
;;;
;;; THE NOTES' RESULTS (Thayer, Construction of Measures, Ch. 4 Sec. 3, pp. 38-40):
;;;   metric-outer-measure-finite-separated  Lemma 4.27, Eq. (93)
;;;   metric-outer-measure-separated-sum     Lemma 4.27, Eq. (92)
;;;   metric-outer-layers-limit              Lemma 4.28: E_n = {x : d(x, F) >= 1/(n+1)}
;;;                                          nondecreasing, and mu*(A n (X \ F)) is the ESUP
;;;                                          of mu*(A n E_n) (the notes' limit, (96))
;;;   metric-outer-closed-measurable         the proof of Prop. 4.26 (closed sets)
;;;   metric-outer-open-measurable           open sets (complements)
;;;   borel-sets-caratheodory-measurable     Prop. 4.26
;;;   lebesgue-metric-outer-measure          Lebesgue outer measure is a metric outer
;;;                                          measure on RR-MS (not proved in the notes)
;;;   lebesgue-borel-measurable              the corollary: Borel sets of the line
;;;   lebesgue-intervals-measurable          CCINT, OOINT, COINT, OCINT(a, b) for real a, b
;;;   lebesgue-left-ray-measurable, lebesgue-right-ray-measurable   (-inf, c], [c, +inf)
;;;   lebesgue-open-left-ray-measurable, lebesgue-open-right-ray-measurable
;;;
;;; THE BRICKS: pt-set-dist-{unfold,ge-intro,ge-elim}, set-dist-{unfold,pos-intro,pos-elim}
;;; (a positive real separates the sets), set-dist-union-pos; metric-outer-measure-prefix-
;;; separated (the induction behind (93)); the layers: dist-layer-{mem-elim,mem-intro,mono,
;;; disjoint,cover,gap,ring-cover,set-sep}, nn-recip-succ-gap, rr-mul-succ-distrib; the even
;;; and odd rings: dist-ring-{even,odd}-sum-le; sums in [0, +inf]: rps-prefix-mono,
;;; rps-prefix-even-odd, esum-nn-even-odd-le, rps-prefix-shift-le, esum-nn-tail-small (THE
;;; SMALL TAIL), esum-nn-superadditive; the line: ccint-inter-{left,right}-ray(-all),
;;; ccint-empty-of-lt, empty-inter-{left,right}-ray, ccint-in-closed-intervals, lebesgue-cut,
;;; lebesgue-split-le, grid-cell-{cover,close}-{nonneg,nonpos}; sigma-algebras:
;;; sigma-algebra-binary-{union,inter}, sigma-algebra-sep-meets; and
;;; metric-outer-of-separating-sets (an outer measure is metric when separated sets are
;;; always split by a measurable set).
;;;
;;; WHERE THE PROOFS LEAVE THE NOTES (findings of the report):
;;;   * 4.27: the induction for (93) must carry the positive distance of the prefix union
;;;     U_(k<n) E_k from every later E_j (set-dist-union-pos); the notes say "by induction".
;;;   * 4.28: E_n uses 1/(n+1) (n in NN from 0).  The case split is on L = sup mu*(A n E_n)
;;;     finite or not, rather than on the finiteness of (98): with L finite the even and the
;;;     odd ring sums are each <= L (4.27 (93) on the rings, every prefix union lies in A n
;;;     E_2n), so (98) is finite, and the tail estimate needs the small-tail lemma; the gap
;;;     d(S_2k, S_2l) > 0 is the gap lemma between E_j and the points off E_(j+1), whose
;;;     positivity is 1/(j+1) - 1/(j+2) > 0 (nn-recip-succ-gap; no such lemma was in the tree).
;;;     The covering X \ F = E_n u U_(k>=n) S_k uses the least index of a layer containing x.
;;;   * 4.26: (103) is proved through esup-eplus-le with the constant sequence mu*(A n F).
;;;   * The Lebesgue part departs from the brief's route (cut every covering interval and
;;;     re-index): the half-lines are measurable directly (one cut point, two covers on the
;;;     same index), and separated A, B are split by the measurable union of the grid cells
